ba0788bcca584c136a492878bc5224fc7d5c598b6d98dc84ef83621b/2a1d8a89f00ace1b1ddd51d9e5ff705929e76b6edeee97f394d666550954368a" "q-340908/selection/ma-v1/071435deba0788bcca584c136a492878bc5224fc7d5c598b6d98dc84ef83621b/c820fc75b83ab642062caecaa665e20c3efdace607db6b590468cae51c449859" "q-340908/selection/ma-v1/071435deba0788bcca584c136a492878bc5224fc7d5c598b6d98dc84ef83621b/54260034644bf2398d955582fcfe14cc95cc4887ad666376368d8327ed5e3102"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-340908"] :question/problem "Which of the following functions are piecewise continuous on $x\\in [-1,1].$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{x},\\,g(x)={\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<0 \\\\ 1,\\, & x\\ge 0,\\end{aligned}\\,h(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous on $x\\in [a,b]$ if\n\n- it is continuous on $[a,b]$ except at finitely many points, and\n\n- at each point of discontinuity, the one-sided limits exist and are finite.\n\nIn other words, there can only be finitely many discontinuities, and each of them must be either a point or a jump discontinuity.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not piecewise continuous over $x\\in [-1,1]$ since the one-sided limits at $x=0$ are infinite:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty ,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)=+\\infty \n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [-1,1]$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [-1,1]$ except at the point $x=0,$ where the one-sided limits exist and are finite:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}g(x)=0,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}g(x)=1\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [-1,1]$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [-1,1].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$g$ and $h$ only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-340908/selection/ma-v1/071435deba0788bcca584c136a492878bc5224fc7d5c598b6d98dc84ef83621b"] :db/ensure [:questi