32" :question/id #uuid "ce2b59e6-6228-5c20-b8cf-7a13bfb991fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-341432" :question/problem "Which of the following functions are piecewise continuous on $x\\in [0,\\pi ].$\n\n$$\nf(x)=-\\frac{2}{x+1},\\,g(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\, & x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ -1,\\, & x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\end{aligned}\\,h(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous on $x\\in [a,b]$ if\n\n- it is continuous on $[a,b]$ except at finitely many points, and\n\n- at each point of discontinuity, the one-sided limits exist and are finite.\n\nIn other words, there can only be finitely many discontinuities, and each of them must be either a point or a jump discontinuity.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\pi ]$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [0,\\pi ].$\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\pi ]$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [0,\\pi ]$ except at the point $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ where the one-sided limits exist and are finite:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\pi /{2}^{-}}g(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\pi /{2}^{+}}g(x)=0\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\pi ]$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [0,\\pi ].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$f,g,$ and $h.$\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-341432/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "35b757ed-e2b3-5ab2-8cef-6ec9ef7bb7e8"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-341432"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-341438/selection/bb292cda762e5b8cdec132334ba43c76a2cfaab9cfffc89bd50f767fb9b03a74" :answer/id #uuid "cc85fd89-6175-5ae6-9cdd-386e45657504" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-341438/selection/c820fc75b83ab642062caecaa665e20c3efdace607db6b590468cae51c449859" :answer/id #uuid "c359d4c5-6252-5792-9161-b2e00f6cb4b1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f$ and $h$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-341438/selection/8179e352607fde1d20643aaf2dd5550f91f25ca3d0bbbef167f92cf9dc7a168e" :answer/id #uuid "d9eb153e-c748-5eae-a6e4-64964b5d01a0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$g$ and $h$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-341438/selection/54260034644bf2398d955582fcfe14cc95cc4887ad666376368d8327ed5e3102" :answer/id #uuid "433f8338-e09a-588d-845b-33eb4b487d15" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$g$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-341438/selection/3a3755feb1a05cec3176c3d2cc45a265049aa4bdd6b69f00387a116663369c21" :answer/id #uuid "d0521f22-b354-56a2-a59c-1cd9a2088a73" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f,g,$ and $h$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-341438/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "2cdcf774-d30a-552b-9bfd-cf8b6fe0b5b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-341438/selection/8179e352607fde1d20643aaf2dd5550f91f25ca3d0bbbef167f92cf9dc7a168e" :answer-field/choices ["q-341438/selection/bb292cda762e5b8cdec132334ba43c76a2cfaab9cfffc89bd50f767fb9b03a74" "q-341438/selection/c820fc75b83ab642062caecaa665e20c3efdace607db6b590468cae51c449859" "q-341438/selection/8179e352607fde1d20643aaf2dd5550f91f25ca3d0bbbef167f92cf9dc7a168e" "q-341438/selection/54260034644bf2398d955582fcfe14cc95cc4887ad666376368d8327ed5e3102" "q-341438/selection/3a3755feb1a05cec3176c3d2cc45a265049aa4bdd6b69f00387a116663369c21"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-341438" :question/id #uuid "56e65834-9763-5249-955e-9f38bddbe62f" :question/math-academy-id "q-341438" :question/problem "Which of the following functions are piecewise continuous on $x\\in [0,2].$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{x-1},\\,g(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,h(x)={\\begin{aligned}2x+1,\\, & x<1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+1,\\, & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous on $x\\in [a,b]$ if\n\n- it is continuous on $[a,b]$ except at finitely many points, and\n\n- at each point of discontinuity, the one-sided limits exist and are finite.\n\nIn other words, there can only be finitely many discontinuities, and each of them must be either a point or a jump discontinuity.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,2]$ since the one-sided limits at $x=1$ are infinite:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty ,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)=+\\infty \n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,2]$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [0,2].$\n\n- The function $h(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,2]$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [0,2]$ except at the point $x=1,$ where the one-sided limits exist and are finite:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}h(x)=3,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}h(x)=2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$g$ and $h$ only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-341438/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "35b757ed-e2b3-5ab2-8cef-6ec9ef7bb7e8"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-341438"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-341635/selection/0c1ca63c3c6c805ef06c7bcf31ae0d5219aad8d8f314916c203a48f8e14c7d02" :answer/id #uuid "72bb0deb-42f7-5912-893c-06ed58b1d267" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f$ and $g$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-341635/selection/03748f52aea6637ec4ac8e6115a0813c94904a56cb803618e611dd65fd847541" :answer/id #uuid "9d14883b-b7b3-5512-ac4b-fcdc86c44a3f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f,g$ and $h$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-341635/selection/54260034644bf2398d955582fcfe14cc95cc4887ad666376368d8327ed5e3102" :answer/id #uuid "b1ef8215-8738-5de9-b770-5908d381ff8d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$g$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-341635/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25" :answer/id #uuid "beb20866-a212-5e52-aed7-ed4fab877472" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-341635/selection/2a1d8a89f00ace1b1ddd51d9e5ff705929e76b6edeee97f394d666550954368a" :answer/id #uuid "3c55abc0-efca-56e7-a200-499e76f44b17" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$h$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-341635/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "a053d49a-b56d-5386-ac68-3072b6fef1c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-341635/selection/0c1ca63c3c6c805ef06c7bcf31ae0d5219aad8d8f314916c203a48f8e14c7d02" :answer-field/choices ["q-341635/selection/0c1ca63c3c6c805ef06c7bcf31ae0d5219aad8d8f314916c203a48f8e14c7d02" "q-341635/selection/03748f52aea6637ec4ac8e6115a0813c94904a56cb803618e611dd65fd847541" "q-341635/selection/54260034644bf2398d955582fcfe14cc95cc4887ad666376368d8327ed5e3102" "q-341635/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25" "q-341635/selection/2a1d8a89f00ace1b1ddd51d9e5ff705929e76b6edeee97f394d666550954368a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-341635" :question/id #uuid "f17bfd4a-9fbe-5a93-8302-50f37ddefec2" :question/math-academy-id "q-341635" :question/problem "Which of the following functions are piecewise continuous on $x\\in [-5,\\infty ).$\n\n$$\nf(x)=⌈x⌉,\\,g(x)=u(x+1)+u(x+4),\\,h(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\n$$\n\nNote that $u(x)$ is the unit step function, and $⌈x⌉$ is the ceiling function." :question/worked-solution "Suppose $I⊆R.$ A function $f:I\\to R$ is piecewise continuous on $I$ if it is piecewise continuous on every closed and bounded subinterval $[a,b]⊂I.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [-5,\\infty ).$ The function has jump discontinuities at $x=…,-2,-1,0,1,2,…,$ and any closed interval $[a,b]⊂[-5,\\infty )$ will contain finitely many discontinuities.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042449/assets/788b349598dcabebf92609183b3c48851d75bb11e22efff881a208e58be510b9.png)\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [-5,\\infty )$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [-5,\\infty )$ except at the points $x=-4$ and $x=-1,$ where the one-sided limits exist and are finite:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{4}^{-}}g(x)=0,\\,{lim}_{x\\to -{4}^{+}}g(x)=1\n$$\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{-}}g(x)=1,\\,{lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{+}}g(x)=2\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)$ is not piecewise continuous over $x\\in [-5,\\infty ).$ Consider the closed subinterval $x\\in [0,\\pi ].$ Notice that the one-sided limits at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ are infinite:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\pi /{2}^{-}}h(x)=+\\infty ,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\pi /{2}^{+}}h(x)=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$f$ and $g$ only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-341635/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5985dfc1-a05b-5ac9-a33d-8398478e252c"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-341635"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-341641/selection/03748f52aea6637ec4ac8e6115a0813c94904a56cb803618e611dd65fd847541" :answer/id #uuid "cbb21cac-8a3a-589f-a442-61e655d4efe5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f,g$ and $h$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-341641/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25" :answer/id #uuid "0025b9f2-fc9f-5b87-80b3-7902c7d2b23b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-341641/selection/8179e352607fde1d20643aaf2dd5550f91f25ca3d0bbbef167f92cf9dc7a168e" :answer/id #uuid "49913dc7-76dc-5c84-8135-557d231a9cd4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$g$ and $h$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-341641/selection/54260034644bf2398d955582fcfe14cc95cc4887ad666376368d8327ed5e3102" :answer/id #uuid "43b65c21-8755-5172-83bc-4d8e79696fb7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$g$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-341641/selection/2a1d8a89f00ace1b1ddd51d9e5ff705929e76b6edeee97f394d666550954368a" :answer/id #uuid "0c44978c-148d-5641-b878-5aa25d60cc63" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$h$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-341641/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "5f2d6f26-3d1c-5d25-8a9e-2f386af96c32" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-341641/selection/8179e352607fde1d20643aaf2dd5550f91f25ca3d0bbbef167f92cf9dc7a168e" :answer-field/choices ["q-341641/selection/03748f52aea6637ec4ac8e6115a0813c94904a56cb803618e611dd65fd847541" "q-341641/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25" "q-341641/selection/8179e352607fde1d20643aaf2dd5550f91f25ca3d0bbbef167f92cf9dc7a168e" "q-341641/selection/54260034644bf2398d955582fcfe14cc95cc4887ad666376368d8327ed5e3102" "q-341641/selection/2a1d8a89f00ace1b1ddd51d9e5ff705929e76b6edeee97f394d666550954368a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-341641" :question/id #uuid "775a4c43-c1ba-53d6-a908-1401a73813be" :question/math-academy-id "q-341641" :question/problem "Which of the following functions are piecewise continuous on $x\\in [-6,\\infty ).$\n\n$$\nf(x)=u(x-1)+u\\,(x-\\frac{1}{2})+u\\,(x-\\frac{1}{3})+⋯,\\,g(x)=u(x+4)+u(x-6),\\,h(x)={\\begin{aligned}-1,\\, & ⌈x⌉\\text{ is even} \\\\ 1,\\, & ⌈x⌉\\text{ is odd}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $u(x)$ is the unit step function, and $⌈x⌉$ is the ceiling function." :question/worked-solution "Suppose $I⊆R.$ A function $f:I\\to R$ is piecewise continuous on $I$ if it is piecewise continuous on every closed and bounded subinterval $[a,b]⊂I.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not piecewise continuous over $x\\in [-6,\\infty ).$ Consider the closed subinterval $x\\in [0,1].$ Notice that the function has jump discontinuities at $x=1,\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{3},…,$ so it has infinitely many discontinuities on this closed interval.\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [-6,\\infty )$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [-6,\\infty )$ except at the points $x=-4$ and $x=6,$ where the one-sided limits exist and are finite:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{4}^{-}}g(x)=0,\\,{lim}_{x\\to -{4}^{+}}g(x)=1\n$$\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {6}^{-}}g(x)=1,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {6}^{+}}g(x)=2\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [-6,\\infty ).$ The function has jump discontinuities at $x=0,1,2,…,$ and any closed interval $[a,b]⊂[-6,\\infty )$ will contain finitely many discontinuities.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042449/assets/9e447c0672628ce8b304726f36885f18661bbba790ae34ca806aaae95a465bbb.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$g$ and $h$ only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-341641/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5985dfc1-a05b-5ac9-a33d-8398478e252c"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-341641"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-341637/selection/8179e352607fde1d20643aaf2dd5550f91f25ca3d0bbbef167f92cf9dc7a168e" :answer/id #uuid "ec95bfbb-192e-5bd3-a4f8-7e18b84aef43" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$g$ and $h$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-341637/selection/54260034644bf2398d955582fcfe14cc95cc4887ad666376368d8327ed5e3102" :answer/id #uuid "99d65772-98f5-57c3-99ae-9a4c297f11e9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$g$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-341637/selection/2a1d8a89f00ace1b1ddd51d9e5ff705929e76b6edeee97f394d666550954368a" :answer/id #uuid "cd86e7bf-25f2-5d6e-be47-8c99813978bb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$h$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-341637/selection/03748f52aea6637ec4ac8e6115a0813c94904a56cb803618e611dd65fd847541" :answer/id #uuid "704807ba-e54e-5e38-838b-e7852ad437d4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f,g$ and $h$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-341637/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25" :answer/id #uuid "66e4cd81-2207-5339-8487-c20c00b5562f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-341637/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "3c64e665-9f5e-53d6-9524-2806ad02238e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-341637/selection/8179e352607fde1d20643aaf2dd5550f91f25ca3d0bbbef167f92cf9dc7a168e" :answer-field/choices ["q-341637/selection/8179e352607fde1d20643aaf2dd5550f91f25ca3d0bbbef167f92cf9dc7a168e" "q-341637/selection/54260034644bf2398d955582fcfe14cc95cc4887ad666376368d8327ed5e3102" "q-341637/selection/2a1d8a89f00ace1b1ddd51d9e5ff705929e76b6edeee97f394d666550954368a" "q-341637/selection/03748f52aea6637ec4ac8e6115a0813c94904a56cb803618e611dd65fd847541" "q-341637/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-341637" :question/id #uuid "017a0ad4-409b-5227-b0c5-d2f78357fb9f" :question/math-academy-id "q-341637" :question/problem "Which of the following functions are piecewise continuous on $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x,\\,g(x)=⌊\\frac{x}{3}⌋,\\,h(x)={\\begin{aligned}1,\\, & ⌈x⌉\\text{ is even} \\\\ -1,\\, & ⌈x⌉\\text{ is odd}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $u(x)$ is the unit step function, and $⌈x⌉$ is the ceiling function." :question/worked-solution "Suppose $I⊆R.$ A function $f:I\\to R$ is piecewise continuous on $I$ if it is piecewise continuous on every closed and bounded subinterval $[a,b]⊂I.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ Consider the closed subinterval $x\\in [0,2\\pi ].$ Notice that the one-sided limits at $x=\\pi $ are infinite: \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{-}}f(x)=+\\infty ,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{+}}f(x)=-\\infty .\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ The function has jump discontinuities at $x=…,-6,-3,0,3,6,…,$ and any closed interval $[a,b]⊂[0,\\infty )$ will contain finitely many discontinuities.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042449/assets/370ce6bc297340f28b1e5bcd4a7e0ca3ce5de6edac660e143ac70ba615184765.png)\n\n- The function $h(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ The function has jump discontinuities at $x=0,1,2,…,$ and any closed interval $[a,b]⊂[0,\\infty )$ will contain finitely many discontinuities.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042449/assets/9b7758701595db784d2d9aeb59cc823316501748976ccbed41980fa29ad0a892.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$g$ and $h$ only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-341637/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5985dfc1-a05b-5ac9-a33d-8398478e252c"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-341637"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-341628/selection/2a1d8a89f00ace1b1ddd51d9e5ff705929e76b6edeee97f394d666550954368a" :answer/id #uuid "89163003-3e5b-5294-b3a3-a0bedd8ba979" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$h$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-341628/selection/03748f52aea6637ec4ac8e6115a0813c94904a56cb803618e611dd65fd847541" :answer/id #uuid "867f4292-2071-5459-8007-1b19d36a7769" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f,g$ and $h$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-341628/selection/0c1ca63c3c6c805ef06c7bcf31ae0d5219aad8d8f314916c203a48f8e14c7d02" :answer/id #uuid "f0b6ae82-1725-576a-ac31-2a95d891c4a1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f$ and $g$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-341628/selection/c820fc75b83ab642062caecaa665e20c3efdace607db6b590468cae51c449859" :answer/id #uuid "1d8e37c0-071c-5712-9f3f-95e09c403fd2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f$ and $h$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-341628/selection/54260034644bf2398d955582fcfe14cc95cc4887ad666376368d8327ed5e3102" :answer/id #uuid "0a4f3bac-cc5d-5a19-8caf-a8f25aa2d3ca" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$g$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-341628/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "3fa9ff52-cb36-5b07-86f2-14613297dc05" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-341628/selection/c820fc75b83ab642062caecaa665e20c3efdace607db6b590468cae51c449859" :answer-field/choices ["q-341628/selection/2a1d8a89f00ace1b1ddd51d9e5ff705929e76b6edeee97f394d666550954368a" "q-341628/selection/03748f52aea6637ec4ac8e6115a0813c94904a56cb803618e611dd65fd847541" "q-341628/selection/0c1ca63c3c6c805ef06c7bcf31ae0d5219aad8d8f314916c203a48f8e14c7d02" "q-341628/selection/c820fc75b83ab642062caecaa665e20c3efdace607db6b590468cae51c449859" "q-341628/selection/54260034644bf2398d955582fcfe14cc95cc4887ad666376368d8327ed5e3102"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-341628" :question/id #uuid "e7cb3010-6287-51e3-9b8c-18c0e77a0e69" :question/math-academy-id "q-341628" :question/problem "Which of the following functions are piecewise continuous on $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\n$$\nf(x)=u(x-2)+u(x-6),\\,g(x)=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x,\\,h(x)=⌊\\frac{x}{2}⌋\n$$\n\nNote that $u(x)$ is the unit step function, and $⌊x⌋$ is the floor function." :question/worked-solution "Suppose $I⊆R.$ A function $f:I\\to R$ is piecewise continuous on $I$ if it is piecewise continuous on every closed and bounded subinterval $[a,b]⊂I.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ except at the points $x=6$ and $x=2,$ where the one-sided limits exist and are finite:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {6}^{-}}f(x)=0,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {6}^{+}}f(x)=1\n$$\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x)=1,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)=2\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is not piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ Consider the closed subinterval $x\\in [0,\\pi ].$ Notice that the one-sided limits at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ are infinite:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\pi /{2}^{-}}g(x)=+\\infty ,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\pi /{2}^{+}}g(x)=-\\infty .\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ The function has jump discontinuities at $x=…,-4,-2,0,2,4,…,$ and any closed interval $[a,b]⊂[0,\\infty )$ will contain finitely many discontinuities.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042449/assets/b9ffe76f612fdc577d64526ebc3322b81a87ec3856040c1c8edccb1be3b6fe5d.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$f$ and $h$ only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-341628/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5985dfc1-a05b-5ac9-a33d-8398478e252c"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-341628"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-341646/selection/2a1d8a89f00ace1b1ddd51d9e5ff705929e76b6edeee97f394d666550954368a" :answer/id #uuid "0218821f-31d1-5b08-961d-1565e1257e6b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$h$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-341646/selection/8179e352607fde1d20643aaf2dd5550f91f25ca3d0bbbef167f92cf9dc7a168e" :answer/id #uuid "8e9f4149-9bc7-5fc8-a259-1b51d502b13d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$g$ and $h$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-341646/selection/a9c4dd2d84043d26472bccd93e41bcb8a4cb6f21249322cef832990cc767c285" :answer/id #uuid "30c7f37d-585d-5706-af93-b2f11c51ee0e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f$ and $h$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-341646/selection/54260034644bf2398d955582fcfe14cc95cc4887ad666376368d8327ed5e3102" :answer/id #uuid "04369ebd-82bb-5139-b0e4-fa0ae64ff8e0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$g$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-341646/selection/bb292cda762e5b8cdec132334ba43c76a2cfaab9cfffc89bd50f767fb9b03a74" :answer/id #uuid "bf690455-290a-51fa-99f6-095fab8bc757" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-341646/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "685c626e-eee7-531b-bef7-6c143fe01f36" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-341646/selection/2a1d8a89f00ace1b1ddd51d9e5ff705929e76b6edeee97f394d666550954368a" :answer-field/choices ["q-341646/selection/2a1d8a89f00ace1b1ddd51d9e5ff705929e76b6edeee97f394d666550954368a" "q-341646/selection/8179e352607fde1d20643aaf2dd5550f91f25ca3d0bbbef167f92cf9dc7a168e" "q-341646/selection/a9c4dd2d84043d26472bccd93e41bcb8a4cb6f21249322cef832990cc767c285" "q-341646/selection/54260034644bf2398d955582fcfe14cc95cc4887ad666376368d8327ed5e3102" "q-341646/selection/bb292cda762e5b8cdec132334ba43c76a2cfaab9cfffc89bd50f767fb9b03a74"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-341646" :question/id #uuid "d93c223a-1777-5c0c-9444-49fbe02db738" :question/math-academy-id "q-341646" :question/problem "Which of the following functions are piecewise continuous on $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\n$$\nf(x)=u\\,(x-\\frac{1}{2})+u\\,(x-\\frac{1}{4})+u\\,(x-\\frac{1}{8})+⋯,\\,g(x)=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x,\\,h(x)=u(x-2)+u(x-5)\n$$\n\nNote that $u(x)$ is the unit step function." :question/worked-solution "Suppose $I⊆R.$ A function $f:I\\to R$ is piecewise continuous on $I$ if it is piecewise continuous on every closed and bounded subinterval $[a,b]⊂I.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ Consider the closed subinterval $x\\in [0,\\frac{1}{2}].$ Notice that the function has jump discontinuities at $x=\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{1}{8},\\frac{1}{16},…,$ so it has infinitely many discontinuities on this closed interval.\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is not piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ Consider the closed subinterval $x\\in [0,\\pi ].$ Notice that the one-sided limits at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ are infinite:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\pi /{2}^{-}}g(x)=+\\infty ,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\pi /{2}^{+}}g(x)=-\\infty .\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ except at the points $x=2$ and $x=5,$ where the one-sided limits exist and are finite:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}h(x)=0,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}h(x)=1\n$$\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {5}^{-}}h(x)=1,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {5}^{+}}h(x)=2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$h$ only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-341646/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5985dfc1-a05b-5ac9-a33d-8398478e252c"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-341646"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-394144/selection/9af10456e8aaa200b7ffe625b2da3a488db9dd53569041e400767aa22135d465" :answer/id #uuid "d6225cfb-3351-53f1-b33e-e2221fe67990" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f,$ $g,$ and $h$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-394144/selection/c820fc75b83ab642062caecaa665e20c3efdace607db6b590468cae51c449859" :answer/id #uuid "ec97f75f-035c-5e34-afb8-f6f72bfcc6ea" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f$ and $h$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-394144/selection/bb292cda762e5b8cdec132334ba43c76a2cfaab9cfffc89bd50f767fb9b03a74" :answer/id #uuid "01baef1e-ee03-5685-80b6-41bb726fae8c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-394144/selection/54260034644bf2398d955582fcfe14cc95cc4887ad666376368d8327ed5e3102" :answer/id #uuid "805ac4f2-938b-50bd-b5ef-8668ea170e44" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$g$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-394144/selection/ade903f23dee9b3d3ae4a03c52779b644cf55dccec6141fd3fa7e38282ebac00" :answer/id #uuid "a84bc3d1-85a9-599f-96bc-97a6c880be40" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of the functions" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-394144/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "aad52def-c29b-5e93-924a-0e86a99ada14" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-394144/selection/ade903f23dee9b3d3ae4a03c52779b644cf55dccec6141fd3fa7e38282ebac00" :answer-field/choices ["q-394144/selection/9af10456e8aaa200b7ffe625b2da3a488db9dd53569041e400767aa22135d465" "q-394144/selection/c820fc75b83ab642062caecaa665e20c3efdace607db6b590468cae51c449859" "q-394144/selection/bb292cda762e5b8cdec132334ba43c76a2cfaab9cfffc89bd50f767fb9b03a74" "q-394144/selection/54260034644bf2398d955582fcfe14cc95cc4887ad666376368d8327ed5e3102" "q-394144/selection/ade903f23dee9b3d3ae4a03c52779b644cf55dccec6141fd3fa7e38282ebac00"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-394144" :question/id #uuid "c38d4e5c-b16a-5c09-92de-bf8db23d9d51" :question/math-academy-id "q-394144" :question/problem "Which of the following functions are piecewise smooth on $x\\in [-1,1]?$\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}2x-\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}, & x<0 \\\\ x+\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{2}}, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\\,,\\,g(x)=4x-\\sqrt[7]{{x}^{3}},\\,h(x)=3{x}^{2}+\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x)$ is **piecewise smooth** on $x\\in [a,b]$ if\n\n- $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous on $[a,b],$ and\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists and is continuous except possibly at finitely many points, where its one-sided limits exist and are finite.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not piecewise smooth on $x\\in [-1,1].$ We split the interval at $x=0.$ On the open intervals $(-1,0)$ and $(0,1),$ we have\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}2x-{x}^{2/3}, & x<0 \\\\ x+{x}^{2/5}, & x>0\\end{aligned}\\,,\n$$\n\nso\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={\\begin{aligned}2-\\frac{2}{3{x}^{1/3}}, & x<0 \\\\ 1+\\frac{2}{5{x}^{3/5}}, & x>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhile this derivative is continuous on the open pieces $(-1,0)$ and $(0,1),$ the limits of the derivative as $x\\to 0$ are not finite. In fact, the derivative becomes unbounded near $x=0.$ Thus, the condition for finite limits at the endpoints is not met.\n\n- The function $g(x)=4x-\\sqrt[7]{{x}^{3}}$ is not piecewise smooth on $x\\in [-1,1].$ We split the interval at $x=0$ into the two open intervals $(-1,0)$ and $(0,1).$ For $x\\ne 0,$\n\n$$\ng(x)=4x-{x}^{3/7},\n$$\n\nso\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(x)=4-\\frac{3}{7{x}^{4/7}}.\n$$\n\nWhile this derivative is continuous on the open pieces $(-1,0)$ and $(0,1),$ the limits of the derivative as $x\\to 0$ are not finite. In fact, the derivative becomes unbounded near $x=0.$ Thus, the condition for finite limits at the endpoints is not met.\n\n- The function $h(x)=3{x}^{2}+\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}}$ is not piecewise smooth on $x\\in [-1,1].$ We split the interval at $x=0$ into the two open intervals $(-1,0)$ and $(0,1).$ For $x\\ne 0,$\n\n$$\nh(x)=3{x}^{2}+{x}^{3/5},\n$$\n\nso\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=6x+\\frac{3}{5{x}^{2/5}}.\n$$\n\nWhile this derivative is continuous on the open pieces $(-1,0)$ and $(0,1),$ the limits of the derivative as $x\\to 0$ are not finite. In fact, the derivative becomes unbounded near $x=0.$ Thus, the condition for finite limits at the endpoints is not met.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the functions.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-394144/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "kp-fcf6bdc9-3628-5a16-88d6-80be1670c38d" :knowledge-point/questions ["q-394144"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-394128/selection/9af10456e8aaa200b7ffe625b2da3a488db9dd53569041e400767aa22135d465" :answer/id #uuid "e452dd22-2362-5a98-b894-2cc8597e9633" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f,$ $g,$ and $h$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-394128/selection/bb292cda762e5b8cdec132334ba43c76a2cfaab9cfffc89bd50f767fb9b03a74" :answer/id #uuid "b02b12f1-261c-5678-8156-0f671af81881" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-394128/selection/8179e352607fde1d20643aaf2dd5550f91f25ca3d0bbbef167f92cf9dc7a168e" :answer/id #uuid "58f5b303-9f8f-5ac6-917e-062734c7d83b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$g$ and $h$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-394128/selection/54260034644bf2398d955582fcfe14cc95cc4887ad666376368d8327ed5e3102" :answer/id #uuid "c7e23482-d62f-59b3-b547-a3c1ed796551" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$g$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-394128/selection/0c1ca63c3c6c805ef06c7bcf31ae0d5219aad8d8f314916c203a48f8e14c7d02" :answer/id #uuid "bc981bd2-3ac7-5462-a2ba-ecd736bdbbc3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f$ and $g$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-394128/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "b1d1ae7b-abfe-5f90-98ac-d77131e11a10" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-394128/selection/bb292cda762e5b8cdec132334ba43c76a2cfaab9cfffc89bd50f767fb9b03a74" :answer-field/choices ["q-394128/selection/9af10456e8aaa200b7ffe625b2da3a488db9dd53569041e400767aa22135d465" "q-394128/selection/bb292cda762e5b8cdec132334ba43c76a2cfaab9cfffc89bd50f767fb9b03a74" "q-394128/selection/8179e352607fde1d20643aaf2dd5550f91f25ca3d0bbbef167f92cf9dc7a168e" "q-394128/selection/54260034644bf2398d955582fcfe14cc95cc4887ad666376368d8327ed5e3102" "q-394128/selection/0c1ca63c3c6c805ef06c7bcf31ae0d5219aad8d8f314916c203a48f8e14c7d02"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-394128" :question/id #uuid "00fff0d6-a0fa-50a2-ab42-de11b65f0e2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-394128" :question/problem "Which of the following functions are piecewise smooth on $x\\in [0,1]?$\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}3x-1, & x<\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 3x+2, & x\\ge \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\\,,\\,g(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{x}),\\,h(x)=\\sqrt[4]{x}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x)$ is **piecewise smooth** on $x\\in [a,b]$ if\n\n- $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous on $[a,b],$ and\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists and is continuous except possibly at finitely many points, where its one-sided limits exist and are finite.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in [0,1].$ We split the interval at $x=\\frac{1}{2}.$ On each open interval, $(0,\\frac{1}{2})$ and $(\\frac{1}{2},1),$ the function is linear. On these intervals,\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}3x-1, & x<\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 3x+2, & x>\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\\,,\n$$\n\nso\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={\\begin{aligned}3, & x<\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 3, & x>\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $f(x)$ and ${f}^{′}(x)$ are piecewise continuous on each open interval, and they both have finite limits at the endpoints of the subintervals.\n\n- The function $g(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{x})$ is not piecewise smooth on $x\\in [0,1].$ For $x\\in (0,1),$\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{x})}{2\\sqrt{x}}.\n$$\n\nWhile this derivative is continuous on the open piece $(0,1),$ the limit of the derivative as $x\\to {0}^{+}$ is not finite. In fact, the derivative becomes unbounded near $x=0.$ Thus, the condition for finite limits at the endpoints is not met.\n\n- The function $h(x)=\\sqrt[4]{x}$ is not piecewise smooth on $x\\in [0,1].$ For $x\\in (0,1),$\n\n$$\nh(x)={x}^{1/4},\n$$\n\nso\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{4{x}^{3/4}}.\n$$\n\nWhile this derivative is continuous on the open piece $(0,1),$ the limit of the derivative as $x\\to {0}^{+}$ is not finite. In fact, the derivative becomes unbounded near $x=0.$ Thus, the condition for finite limits at the endpoints is not met.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$f$ only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-394128/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "kp-fcf6bdc9-3628-5a16-88d6-80be1670c38d" :knowledge-point/questions ["q-394128"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-394142/selection/ade903f23dee9b3d3ae4a03c52779b644cf55dccec6141fd3fa7e38282ebac00" :answer/id #uuid "70c3b7bb-f365-5e62-8d5f-0e349c6d7670" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of the functions" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-394142/selection/2a1d8a89f00ace1b1ddd51d9e5ff705929e76b6edeee97f394d666550954368a" :answer/id #uuid "d7161898-b045-5482-b116-296d60561ff4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$h$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-394142/selection/0c1ca63c3c6c805ef06c7bcf31ae0d5219aad8d8f314916c203a48f8e14c7d02" :answer/id #uuid "055ba5ce-5641-5a7a-b830-f641bb8b3257" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f$ and $g$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-394142/selection/9af10456e8aaa200b7ffe625b2da3a488db9dd53569041e400767aa22135d465" :answer/id #uuid "3d9cc14a-d0d9-51e2-b7c5-e0b750c9b5cd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f,$ $g,$ and $h$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-394142/selection/54260034644bf2398d955582fcfe14cc95cc4887ad666376368d8327ed5e3102" :answer/id #uuid "86fb389f-7d13-5681-a331-7a9c1e324b9e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$g$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-394142/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "fd058a2f-f310-5240-864e-e47a3f8ffc95" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-394142/selection/ade903f23dee9b3d3ae4a03c52779b644cf55dccec6141fd3fa7e38282ebac00" :answer-field/choices ["q-394142/selection/ade903f23dee9b3d3ae4a03c52779b644cf55dccec6141fd3fa7e38282ebac00" "q-394142/selection/2a1d8a89f00ace1b1ddd51d9e5ff705929e76b6edeee97f394d666550954368a" "q-394142/selection/0c1ca63c3c6c805ef06c7bcf31ae0d5219aad8d8f314916c203a48f8e14c7d02" "q-394142/selection/9af10456e8aaa200b7ffe625b2da3a488db9dd53569041e400767aa22135d465" "q-394142/selection/54260034644bf2398d955582fcfe14cc95cc4887ad666376368d8327ed5e3102"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-394142" :question/id #uuid "1a804ed1-fbfc-5aeb-84ec-d84d0298664d" :question/math-academy-id "q-394142" :question/problem "Which of the following functions are piecewise smooth on $x\\in [-1,1]?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}},\\,g(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+1, & x<0 \\\\ \\sqrt[7]{{x}^{3}}, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\\,,\\,h(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}})\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x)$ is **piecewise smooth** on $x\\in [a,b]$ if\n\n- $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous on $[a,b],$ and\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists and is continuous except possibly at finitely many points, where its one-sided limits exist and are finite.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)=\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}}$ is not piecewise smooth on $x\\in [-1,1].$ We split the interval at $x=0$ into the two open intervals $(-1,0)$ and $(0,1).$ For $x\\ne 0,$\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{3/5},\n$$\n\nso\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{3}{5{x}^{2/5}}.\n$$\n\nWhile this derivative is continuous on the open pieces $(-1,0)$ and $(0,1),$ the limits of the derivative as $x\\to 0$ are not finite. In fact, the derivative becomes unbounded near $x=0.$ Thus, the condition for finite limits at the endpoints is not met.\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is not piecewise smooth on $x\\in [-1,1].$ We split the interval at $x=0.$ On each open interval, $(-1,0)$ and $(0,1),$\n\n$$\ng(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+1, & x<0 \\\\ {x}^{3/7}, & x>0\\end{aligned}\\,,\n$$\n\nso\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(x)={\\begin{aligned}2x, & x<0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{7{x}^{4/7}}, & x>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $g(x)$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ are continuous on each open interval, but the limit of ${g}^{′}(x)$ as $x\\to {0}^{+}$ is not finite because $\\frac{3}{7{x}^{4/7}}$ becomes unbounded. Thus, the condition for finite limits at the endpoints is not met.\n\n- The function $h(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}})$ is not piecewise smooth on $x\\in [-1,1].$ We split the interval at $x=0$ into the two open intervals $(-1,0)$ and $(0,1).$ For $x\\ne 0,$\n\n$$\nh(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2/3}),\n$$\n\nso\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2/3})\\cdot \\frac{2}{3{x}^{1/3}}.\n$$\n\nWhile this derivative is continuous on the open pieces $(-1,0)$ and $(0,1),$ the limits of the derivative as $x\\to 0$ are not finite. Because $\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2/3})$ approaches $1$ while $\\frac{2}{3{x}^{1/3}}$ becomes unbounded near $x=0,$ the derivative gets infinitely large. Thus, the condition for finite limits at the endpoints is not met.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the functions.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-394142/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "kp-fcf6bdc9-3628-5a16-88d6-80be1670c38d" :knowledge-point/questions ["q-394142"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-394127/selection/54260034644bf2398d955582fcfe14cc95cc4887ad666376368d8327ed5e3102" :answer/id #uuid "a12733e2-84fd-5c52-b7fe-fdeed5d1c3b0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$g$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-394127/selection/2a1d8a89f00ace1b1ddd51d9e5ff705929e76b6edeee97f394d666550954368a" :answer/id #uuid "a3e5a6ec-5207-5e2e-881e-150f1ca16204" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$h$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-394127/selection/8179e352607fde1d20643aaf2dd5550f91f25ca3d0bbbef167f92cf9dc7a168e" :answer/id #uuid "f48f6d2c-d34f-58b3-abd3-ce1342cab207" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$g$ and $h$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-394127/selection/9af10456e8aaa200b7ffe625b2da3a488db9dd53569041e400767aa22135d465" :answer/id #uuid "42945a4b-b06a-5086-9770-851ba8bf2193" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f,$ $g,$ and $h$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-394127/selection/0c1ca63c3c6c805ef06c7bcf31ae0d5219aad8d8f314916c203a48f8e14c7d02" :answer/id #uuid "c82e9da9-cf38-5f44-8052-4d878745ee6f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f$ and $g$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-394127/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "be1e5df4-878e-5cf6-91eb-3f1054428f49" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-394127/selection/8179e352607fde1d20643aaf2dd5550f91f25ca3d0bbbef167f92cf9dc7a168e" :answer-field/choices ["q-394127/selection/54260034644bf2398d955582fcfe14cc95cc4887ad666376368d8327ed5e3102" "q-394127/selection/2a1d8a89f00ace1b1ddd51d9e5ff705929e76b6edeee97f394d666550954368a" "q-394127/selection/8179e352607fde1d20643aaf2dd5550f91f25ca3d0bbbef167f92cf9dc7a168e" "q-394127/selection/9af10456e8aaa200b7ffe625b2da3a488db9dd53569041e400767aa22135d465" "q-394127/selection/0c1ca63c3c6c805ef06c7bcf31ae0d5219aad8d8f314916c203a48f8e14c7d02"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-394127" :question/id #uuid "db6f9fc1-3b02-5615-8e6c-477c7f6565ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-394127" :question/problem "Which of the following functions are piecewise smooth on $x\\in [-1,1]?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=2x+\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{2}},\\,g(x)={\\begin{aligned}-4x+1, & x<0 \\\\ -4x+3, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\\,,\\,h(x)=x\\sqrt[5]{x}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x)$ is **piecewise smooth** on $x\\in [a,b]$ if\n\n- $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous on $[a,b],$ and\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists and is continuous except possibly at finitely many points, where its one-sided limits exist and are finite.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)=2x+\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{2}}$ is not piecewise smooth on $x\\in [-1,1].$ We split the interval at $x=0$ into the two open intervals $(-1,0)$ and $(0,1).$ For $x\\ne 0,$\n\n$$\nf(x)=2x+{x}^{2/5},\n$$\n\nso\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=2+\\frac{2}{5{x}^{3/5}}.\n$$\n\nWhile this derivative is continuous on the open pieces $(-1,0)$ and $(0,1),$ the limits of the derivative as $x\\to 0$ are not finite. In fact, the derivative becomes unbounded near $x=0.$ Thus, the condition for finite limits at the endpoints is not met.\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in [-1,1].$ We split the interval at $x=0.$ On each open interval, $(-1,0)$ and $(0,1),$ the function is linear. On these intervals,\n\n$$\ng(x)={\\begin{aligned}-4x+1, & x<0 \\\\ -4x+3, & x>0\\end{aligned}\\,,\n$$\n\nso\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(x)={\\begin{aligned}-4, & x<0 \\\\ -4, & x>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $g(x)$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ are piecewise continuous on each open interval, and they both have finite limits at the endpoints of the subintervals.\n\n- The function $h(x)=x\\sqrt[5]{x}$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in [-1,1].$ Since\n\n$$\nh(x)={x}^{6/5},\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=\\frac{6}{5}{x}^{1/5}.\n$$\n\nHere, $h(x)$ and ${h}^{′}(x)$ are continuous on $[-1,1].$ Thus, $h(x)$ is continuously differentiable on the whole interval, and hence piecewise smooth.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$g$ and $h$ only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-394127/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "kp-fcf6bdc9-3628-5a16-88d6-80be1670c38d" :knowledge-point/questions ["q-394127"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-394141/selection/8179e352607fde1d20643aaf2dd5550f91f25ca3d0bbbef167f92cf9dc7a168e" :answer/id #uuid "c9f6b3ec-0960-5e35-b339-8a0645e568c5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$g$ and $h$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-394141/selection/bb292cda762e5b8cdec132334ba43c76a2cfaab9cfffc89bd50f767fb9b03a74" :answer/id #uuid "8b34b6f6-66e9-5c76-95b8-088ce7f730dd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-394141/selection/c820fc75b83ab642062caecaa665e20c3efdace607db6b590468cae51c449859" :answer/id #uuid "c3886322-aff1-5f43-a44b-f41e1789a29f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f$ and $h$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-394141/selection/9af10456e8aaa200b7ffe625b2da3a488db9dd53569041e400767aa22135d465" :answer/id #uuid "88e67d64-9813-5f96-9d36-1cd7751de885" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f,$ $g,$ and $h$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-394141/selection/0c1ca63c3c6c805ef06c7bcf31ae0d5219aad8d8f314916c203a48f8e14c7d02" :answer/id #uuid "15ff1af1-f654-58d6-8147-a0a9261e9aff" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f$ and $g$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-394141/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "0d34aefc-80d4-5958-9e37-1509eb65ee07" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-394141/selection/9af10456e8aaa200b7ffe625b2da3a488db9dd53569041e400767aa22135d465" :answer-field/choices ["q-394141/selection/8179e352607fde1d20643aaf2dd5550f91f25ca3d0bbbef167f92cf9dc7a168e" "q-394141/selection/bb292cda762e5b8cdec132334ba43c76a2cfaab9cfffc89bd50f767fb9b03a74" "q-394141/selection/c820fc75b83ab642062caecaa665e20c3efdace607db6b590468cae51c449859" "q-394141/selection/9af10456e8aaa200b7ffe625b2da3a488db9dd53569041e400767aa22135d465" "q-394141/selection/0c1ca63c3c6c805ef06c7bcf31ae0d5219aad8d8f314916c203a48f8e14c7d02"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-394141" :question/id #uuid "ef15f162-feb7-57d5-b1f9-d007465ef367" :question/math-academy-id "q-394141" :question/problem "Which of the following functions are piecewise smooth on $x\\in [-1,1]?$\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{2}\\sqrt[7]{{x}^{2}},\\,g(x)={x}^{3}+\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{8}},\\,h(x)={\\begin{aligned}5x-3, & x<0 \\\\ 5x+4, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x)$ is **piecewise smooth** on $x\\in [a,b]$ if\n\n- $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous on $[a,b],$ and\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists and is continuous except possibly at finitely many points, where its one-sided limits exist and are finite.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)={x}^{2}\\sqrt[7]{{x}^{2}}$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in [-1,1].$ Since\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{2}\\sqrt[7]{{x}^{2}}={x}^{16/7},\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{16}{7}{x}^{9/7}.\n$$\n\nHere, $f(x)$ and ${f}^{′}(x)$ are continuous on $[-1,1].$ Thus, $f(x)$ is continuously differentiable on the whole interval, and hence piecewise smooth.\n\n- The function $g(x)={x}^{3}+\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{8}}$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in [-1,1].$ Since\n\n$$\ng(x)={x}^{3}+{x}^{8/5},\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(x)=3{x}^{2}+\\frac{8}{5}{x}^{3/5}.\n$$\n\nHere, $g(x)$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ are continuous on $[-1,1].$ Thus, $g(x)$ is continuously differentiable on the whole interval, and hence piecewise smooth.\n\n- The function $h(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in [-1,1].$ We split the interval at $x=0.$ On each open interval, $(-1,0)$ and $(0,1),$ the function is linear. On these intervals,\n\n$$\nh(x)={\\begin{aligned}5x-3, & x<0 \\\\ 5x+4, & x>0\\end{aligned}\\,,\n$$\n\nso\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)={\\begin{aligned}5, & x<0 \\\\ 5, & x>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $h(x)$ and ${h}^{′}(x)$ are piecewise continuous on each open interval, and they both have finite limits at the endpoints of the subintervals.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$f,$ $g,$ and $h.$\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-394141/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "kp-fcf6bdc9-3628-5a16-88d6-80be1670c38d" :knowledge-point/questions ["q-394141"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21841"] :question/problem "Which of the following functions are piecewise continuous on $x\\in [0,\\pi].$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042449/assets/93b55a42a534e4f547b1cab1b700d96b4d5b51656f2090673eb051fefe44221d.png)" :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous on $x\\in [a,b]$ if\n\n- it is continuous on $[a,b]$ except at finitely many points, and\n\n- at each point of discontinuity, the one-sided limits exist and are finite.\n\nIn other words, there can only be finitely many discontinuities, and each of them must be either a point or a jump discontinuity.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\pi]$ since the one-sided limits at $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ are infinite:\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to\\pi/2^-}f(x) = +\\infty, \\qquad \\lim\\limits_{x\\to\\pi/2^+}f(x) = -\\infty\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\pi]$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [0,\\pi].$\n\n- The function $h(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\pi]$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [0,\\pi]$ except at the point $x=1,$ where the one-sided limits exist and are finite:\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to1^-}h(x) = 3, \\qquad \\lim\\limits_{x\\to1^+}h(x) = 4\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$g$ and $h$ only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21843"] :question/problem "Which of the following functions are piecewise continuous on $x\\in [0,\\infty).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}1,\\, & ⌈x⌉\\text{ is even} \\\\ -1,\\, & ⌈x⌉\\text{ is odd}\\end{aligned} \\\\ g(x) & =u(x-1)+u\\,(x-\\frac{1}{2})+u\\,(x-\\frac{1}{3})+⋯ \\\\ h(x) & =⌈\\frac{x}{2}⌉\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $u(x)$ is the unit step function, and $\\lceil x \\rceil$ is the ceiling function." :question/worked-solution "Suppose $I\\subseteq \\mathbb R.$ A function $f: I \\to \\mathbb R$ is piecewise continuous on $I$ if it is piecewise continuous on every closed and bounded subinterval $[a,b]\\subset I.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty).$ The function has jump discontinuities at $x=1,2,3,\\ldots,$ and any closed interval $[a,b]\\subset [0,\\infty)$ will contain finitely many discontinuities.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042449/assets/3ce7aa18de332c3eb1bc57b6bf7c50acf1573717d642c731a18a85f4f9fb813a.png)\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is not piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty).$ Consider the closed subinterval $x\\in [0,1].$ Notice that the function has jump discontinuities at $x=1,\\dfrac12,\\dfrac13,\\dfrac14,\\ldots,$ so it has infinitely many discontinuities on this closed interval.\n\n- The function $h(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty).$ The function has jump discontinuities at $x=0,2,4,\\ldots,$ and any closed interval $[a,b]\\subset [0,\\infty)$ will contain finitely many discontinuities.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042449/assets/9a5cc55dc36927437d16d09b4e491305efebac9d9a8995a741d62b6395a101bd.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$f$ and $h$ only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "e-40653" :question/id #uuid "2444336d-4e2f-5fae-8ec6-dd08736162f1" :question/math-academy-id "e-40653" :question/problem "Which of the following functions are piecewise smooth on ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042449/assets/1146bea1d20a4478e32e2d6c1000dc43dc14ae8a592660fc7618792c8231c671.png)\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042449/assets/c07d756afce881fc79fc5da71aa8fb12a913a6ae244971b6519b9cd6b3c1a9a9.png)" :question/worked-solution "A function ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042449/assets/12a